  "markscheme": {
    "html": "<section class=\"markscheme-part\" data-label=\"13a\"><p><strong>13a</strong></p><p> </p>\n<p>\n<figure data-asset-id=\"fig_02\"><img alt=\"Source markscheme diagram 2\" src=\"asset:fig_02\"/></figure>\n</p>\n<p> </p></section><section class=\"markscheme-part\" data-label=\"13b\"><p><strong>13b</strong></p><p></p>\n<p>The work done per unit charge when moving a positive test charge from infinity to the point.</p>\n<p></p></section><section class=\"markscheme-part\" data-label=\"13c(i)\"><p><strong>13c(i)</strong></p><p></p>\n<p>The work done by the field on the particle is equal to the kinetic energy of the particle.</p>\n<p>Using <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"W = q\\Delta V_{e}\"></span>:</p>\n<p><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"116 \\times 10^{-9} = (-13.8 \\times 10^{-9})(V_{Y} - (-5.30))\"></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"V_{Y} \\approx 3.11\"></span> V</p>\n<p></p></section><section class=\"markscheme-part\" data-label=\"13c(ii)\"><p><strong>13c(ii)</strong></p><p></p>\n<p>Calculating the magnitude of the electric force on the particle:</p>\n<p><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"F = Eq = (124)(13.8 \\times 10^{-9}) \\approx 1.711 \\times 10^{-6}\"></span> N</p>\n<p>Using <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"W = Fs\"></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"116 \\times 10^{-9} = (1.711 \\times 10^{-6})s\"></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"s \\approx 0.0678\"></span> m or <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"6.78\"></span> cm</p>\n<p></p></section>"
  },
  "paper": "paper_2",
  "question": {
    "html": "<p>A particle of charge of <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"-13.8\"></span> nC is placed at point P.</p>\n\n<figure data-asset-id=\"fig_01\"><img alt=\"Source question diagram 1\" src=\"asset:fig_01\"/></figure>\n\n<p>a. On the diagram, draw the electric field lines and the electric equipotentials due to the charge on the particle.</p>\n<p>   [2]</p>\n<p>b. Outline what is meant by electric potential at a point.</p>\n<p>   [2]</p>\n<p>The particle is placed at point X inside a uniform electric field of strength <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"124\"></span> N C<span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"^{-1}\"></span>. When released, the particle moves to point Y. The kinetic energy of the particle at point Y is <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"116\"></span> nJ. The electric potential at X is <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"-5.30\"></span> V.</p>\n<p>c. Calculate the:</p>\n<p>i. electric potential at Y.</p>\n<p>ii . distance between X and Y.</p>\n<p>   [4]</p>"
  },
